-- D6 state foundation, tier C continued: State Board of Education, the
-- 7 states (of 50) where it's a popularly elected multi-member body. Same
-- shape as every migration in this tier -- small hand-verified data, not
-- an ingester. This is a genuinely separate office category from State
-- Board of EQUALIZATION (California's tax-oversight body, migration 071)
-- -- surfaced while researching Texas's Railroad Commission, not part of
-- the taxonomy scanned for that earlier migration.
--
-- Unusually clean verification for this tier: every one of the 7 states
-- has a direct, unambiguous "Selection Method: Elected" field on its own
-- Ballotpedia page -- no party-tag heuristic, no disputed search summary,
-- no false positives to catch this time. A broader search described
-- these 7 as having "all or a majority" of seats elected, which raises
-- one disclosed, NOT ruled out possibility: some other state could have
-- a MINORITY of elected seats on an otherwise-appointed board (e.g. 2 of
-- 11) that wouldn't surface in an "all or majority" framing -- not
-- checked across all 50 states, flagged rather than assumed complete.
-- New Hampshire, California (a separate, Nonpartisan-tagged board from
-- its Board of Equalization), and Oklahoma were independently spot-
-- checked and are Nonpartisan-tagged, consistent with being appointed,
-- though not each individually confirmed via their own Selection Method
-- field the way the 7 states below were.
--
-- Real structural variation, same discipline as every other migration in
-- this tier: Texas (15) and Utah (15) and Kansas (10) are cleanly
-- districted, one single-seat office per district. Colorado (9) has 8
-- districts plus 1 at-large seat. Michigan (8) pools all members under
-- one identical title -- one at_large office, seat_count=8, same
-- multi-seat pattern used throughout this project. Alabama (9 total
-- seats) has a real constitutional ex-officio 9th member -- the
-- Governor, who chairs the board by law -- already modeled as its own
-- Governor office and NOT duplicated here, same pattern as California's
-- Board of Equalization (Controller ex officio) and Montana's combined
-- Insurance Commissioner/Auditor office. Nebraska's 8 members are
-- genuinely popularly elected despite every one being Nonpartisan-tagged
-- in the source -- confirmed via Nebraska's own Selection Method field,
-- same nonpartisan-ballot pattern already seen for Oregon's Labor
-- Commissioner and 4 states' Superintendents in migration 065.
--
-- Term length is real per-state data, not a uniform default: Michigan is
-- 8 years (unusually long, individually confirmed); Colorado is 6 years;
-- Alabama/Kansas/Nebraska/Texas/Utah are 4 years -- Texas's specifically
-- was not independently re-confirmed via its own Selection-Method-style
-- page field the way the other 6 states were, assumed consistent with
-- the state's other 4-year executive offices rather than directly
-- checked.



